WEBVTT

00:00:00.146 --> 00:00:05.882
Retrieval evaluation is necessary, but the final answer also needs evaluation.

00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:10.737
Groundedness asks whether the response stays within the retrieved evidence.

00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:14.867
Correctness asks whether the content is factually right.

00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:19.122
Completeness asks whether important information was omitted.

00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:24.777
Citation precision asks whether the cited source actually supports the claim.

00:00:24.777 --> 00:00:29.727
Helpfulness asks whether the answer is clear enough for a user to act on.

00:00:29.727 --> 00:00:32.482
These dimensions are different. 00:00:32.482 --> 00:00:35.427
A response can be grounded but incomplete.

00:00:35.427 --> 00:00:40.252
It can cite a relevant document but attach the citation to the wrong sentence.

00:00:40.252 --> 00:00:44.232
It can be factually correct but too vague to help the user.

00:00:44.232 --> 00:00:49.897
For a course project, a small human-reviewed rubric is a strong starting point.

00:00:49.897 --> 00:00:56.160
Automated evaluators can be added later, but first define what a good answer means for your application.
